>
  <CatFoodCalc slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How to use the cat food calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Weigh your cat. A bathroom scale works if you weigh yourself with and without the cat.</li>
    <li>Choose the life stage or goal: kitten, neutered adult, intact adult, senior, weight loss, or weight gain.</li>
    <li>Enter the kcal per cup from your food’s label. For canned food, use kcal per can and convert, or use grams.</li>
    <li>Read the daily calorie target and the amount of food, then divide it into meals.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p>
    The calculation starts with Resting Energy Requirement, <code>RER = 70 × (weight in kg)^0.75</code>,
    and multiplies it by a factor for life stage and body-condition goals. This tool uses commonly
    taught factors based on NRC and veterinary nutrition education:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Stage or goal</th><th>Multiplier on RER</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Kitten</td><td>2.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Intact adult</td><td>1.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Neutered adult</td><td>1.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weight loss</td><td>0.8</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Weight gain</td><td>1.4</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>1.1</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These multipliers apply to a healthy cat’s <em>ideal</em> weight for weight-loss calculations. If
    your cat is overweight, using current weight will overshoot; ask your vet for a target weight.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A neutered adult cat at 4.5 kg (about 10 lb): RER = 70 × 4.5^0.75 ≈ 216 kcal. At 1.2 × RER the
    target is about <strong>259 kcal per day</strong>. With a dry food containing 380 kcal per cup,
    that is roughly <strong>0.7 cups</strong> per day, or a little over a third of a cup at each of two
    meals. If you swap in a wet food at 80 kcal per can, the same cat needs about three cans. Many
    owners mix both; just count total calories.
  </p>

  <h2>Wet, dry, and mixed feeding</h2>
  <p>
    Neither format is inherently best. Wet food contributes water, which may help cats that drink
    little, and many wet foods are lower in carbohydrate. Dry food is calorie-dense, convenient, and
    easy to portion with a puzzle feeder. The important parts are that the food is labeled
    <em>complete and balanced</em> for your cat’s life stage under an AAFCO or equivalent standard,
    and that portions are measured. A kitchen gram scale is more accurate than a scoop; scoops can
    vary by 20 percent or more depending on how they are filled.
  </p>

  <h2>Why cats need different food from dogs</h2>
  <p>
    Cats are obligate carnivores. They need taurine, arachidonic acid, preformed vitamin A, and more
    dietary protein than dogs do. Feeding dog food as a primary diet can lead to deficiencies, with
    taurine deficiency historically causing heart disease and blindness. Our{' '}
    <a href="/tools/dog-food-calculator/">dog food calculator</a> uses different multipliers for that
    reason, and the results are not interchangeable. For a broader overview see the{' '}
    <a href="/guides/cat-feeding-guide/">cat feeding guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Monitoring body condition</h2>
  <p>
    Weigh monthly and use a body condition score. An ideal cat has ribs that are easy to feel with a
    light layer of fat, a visible waist from above, and a slight abdominal tuck. A saggy belly pad
    is common in healthy cats and does not by itself mean overweight, but ribs you cannot feel do.
    If weight shifts by more than 10 percent without explanation, or your cat eats much more or less
    than usual, call your veterinarian: weight changes can be early signs of diabetes, hyperthyroidism,
    kidney disease, or dental pain.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Feeding by the bag chart without adjusting for neutering and activity level.</li>
    <li>Forgetting calories from treats, toppers, and shared human food.</li>
    <li>Putting an overweight cat on a crash diet.</li>
    <li>Switching foods abruptly; transition over a week.</li>
    <li>Assuming one cup always equals the same calories across brands.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    You can also estimate your cat’s life stage with the <a href="/tools/cat-age-calculator/">cat age
    calculator</a>, which helps decide whether kitten, adult, or senior formulas are appropriate.
  </p>
